If a data point changes, at least two parity checks have it as one of their selected and will change as well.
so 3 points change, which is an odd number of changes so P4 will recalculate itself and will also change to show there is a problem.

If a parity check changes, only it will be different because the data doesn't depend on it, so we'll know the data is still okay.
It'll still be an odd number of changes so P4 will still change but because its only 1 change before that, we know the data is fine
since if data changed, at least two parities would change alongside it.

if only P4 is different, its the same logic as parity in that the data does not depend on it, we'll note that it's different and promptly ignore
it if everything else is still fine.


